racing innovative new  technologies they're getting rich just  through the power of investments and  compound interest and so on and this is  Thomas Piketty this is sort of the  argument it says this is not this is the  more libertarian in a way version of the  argument that he made in his book  capital in the 21st century where he  basically said you know as societies  sort of as growth slows down and  societies get older and people have  fewer kids great fortunes become more  and more consolidated and rich people  live off ramps and that kind of  inequality is more decadent and more  stagnant and less likely to ultimately  benefit society as a whole then the  inequality you get when you know people  are building the Transcontinental  Railroad and a lot of opportunists are  getting rich off that innovation one of  the things you talk about in the book is  the degree to which a lot of the same  political arguments just continue to be  had over and over again with no real  progress once a country reaches this  level of decadence and I think we can  intuitively see that if you follow  politics a lot of issues that are merely  political footballs you know debt and  deficit just kind of tossing it back and  forth what should the role of the United  States military be around the world do  low taxes on the rich trickle down or do  we need demand sights that you know a  lot of these it's the same arguments  over and over and over again can you  talk about why the repetitive nature of  these arguments is reinforced by the  decadence you write about I mean I think  decadence creates a world in which I  mean among other things there because  there isn't tremendous change going on  there aren't sort of it becomes hard to  win an argument to sort of decisively  win one way or another systems are  really hard to reform and change so you  know you'll get Barack Obama will be  elected president and everyone will say  oh this is you know the new New Deal is  the new Franklin Roosevelt they're going  to have a new age of liberal governance  and he passes Obamacare which is sort of  a jerry-rigged apparatus tacked on to an  existing welfare state passing it is  incredibly difficult and politically  costly and you get a huge backlash and  effectively  his presidency as a legislative force is  over two years in and then immediately  the Tea Party sweeps in and everyone  says ah well it's a new libertarian era  and you know Paul Ryan is the new you  know he's the new great reformer and  he's gonna you know undo the Great  Society or something but that doesn't  actually go anywhere because people  don't particularly want to undo the  Great Society and so you have you know  the libertarian moment quickly turns  into stalemate too so it's a combination  of societies can older they're more  resistant to change big programs  long-standing systems are more  impervious to change and so people argue  at the margins they argue about a 39%  top tax rate versus a 33% top tax rate  even while their rhetoric makes it sound  like you know it's the great clash  between capitalism and socialism and  citizens so that's been the pattern  certainly I think of the last of the  last 25 years or so the question is  whether it still holds or whether  populism on the right and Bernie Sanders  style socialism on the left are actually  going to sort of shift the underlying  dynamics but I don't think they've  actually shifted policy debates yet even  if they've shifted arguments somewhat so  you talk about candidates as a possible  way to shift this what about your  writing that there could be a crisis  moment that actually shakes things up is  the global pandemic we're dealing with  now potentially on the level of what  you're talking about or is it not even  big enough I mean it might not be big  enough well which is one of the things  one of the points I make in the book is  that decadence has all kinds of problems  but sometimes the kind of crisis you  need to really shift a society how do  decadence is so big that it's better to  stick with that consent that embrace the  catastrophe but look what the  coronavirus offers is an opportunity you  know it's it's a whole you know it's a  horrible human tragedy that in its  aftermath there will be an opportunity  to look at all the ways our systems have  failed to look at all the ways our  politics that's become stalemated and  didn't handle the crisis correctly and  say you know maybe we can do something  new and that could lead to you know  you could have an era when people say  okay well we really are going to you  know change how regulations work in the  healthcare sector to try and get more  medical innovation you've dipped in a  world where people say look we spent all  this money in a crisis why can't we  spend more money on public goods when  there isn't a pandemic around and so you  get a shift to the left in public policy  you can imagine a lot of shifts coming  out of this but I do think it's possible  that you know we will have this this  year of turmoil and then we will get a  vaccine and there'll be sort of this  sense of relief that things are back to  normal and people will have sort of gone  deeper into decadence in certain ways  like you know small businesses new  entrepreneurship will have been sort of  smushed down big companies will  benefited the most from the bailout  packages and from the feds policies  until you'll get more consolidation more  big companies that aren't particularly  innovative and so on you know more  people living their lives online and  instead of instead of getting married  and starting families you can imagine a  lot of ways in which this deepens  decadence potentially even as it offers  a chance to pull out of it a couple  weeks ago I interviewed Brian Greene and  one of the things we talked about  because it kind of mixes in well with a  few different things he deals with was  this idea that that I have which  increasingly I think is naive that if  there was some really life-altering  moment like for example intelligent  alien life arrives right something  really transcendent that it would  finally get humans to do away with these  differences that that as you talk about  often or the difference between a thirty  six and a thirty nine point six percent  top tax rate or whatever we would  finally get past all this stuff but I  have to say that the the fiasco that has  been the reaction to coronavirus where  you have people who you know all sorts  of conspiracies and you you all we don't  even have to go into it you know it that  has made me think that even something  like intelligent alien life maybe for a  month would would get us to rethink some  of our differences and then it would all  just kind of go back to normal to the  way things have been I don't know  well I mean the amusing thing is that we  have literally had  sort of footage of not probably not  actual aliens but at the very least UFOs  dropped in the middle of the coronavirus  crisis right you've had the Navy  releasing footage of unidentified flying  objects and you know except for a few  people who I follow on Twitter almost  nobody has cared to be clear that those  are not being presented as evidence of  aliens right but but regardless you  would think that that entire community  would have paid more attention they're  right they're being presented but they  are they're very mysterious objects that  are they'd go beyond I think the sort of  normal range of sort of grainy footage  of UFOs they are probably not aliens but  they certainly are really interesting  and in a different moment you would  imagine that sort of capturing the  imagination of the Internet but really  the Internet is more interested in you  know figuring out ways to blame  everything about the coronavirus on on  people that they dislike we've been  speaking with Ross Douthat  a columnist at the New York Times and  also author recently of the decadent  Society how 